On 2010-08-16, Ray <bear@sonic.net> wrote:
>
> In a public-repository model, doesn't there still have to be someone 
> who says yes or no to individual proposed changes and patches?  

Of course.  Which is why the Spork repository is read-only except for
two people.

> There's a difference between open development and *wide* open 
> development, and it requires rules and politics.  

Not that many rules.

> are points where experienced devs who understand the game can 
> disagree with good arguments on both (or all) sides, and some kind 
> of procedure has to exist for the opinions of one (or some) to be 
> picked over the opinions of others.  

I find that "Because I said so" is often the simplest procedure.  

I do try to listen to the arguments first, but there is a point where it is
simply no longer worth the effort to continue arguing.  Some people will
never agree with you unless you do it precisely their way.  I think
those people should start from nh343 and let me know when their fork is
ready to play -- best of luck.

I don't believe in development by committee, see.  Nor do I believe in
development by rules.  I think both of those do a great job at not
getting things done.  A lot of stuff has got done in Spork since its
inception, and while it's nowhere near as much as I'd hoped, the one
thing I can say is that it hasn't varied from the original vision very
much at all, and I like that.

Not everyone will agree with everything I've done.  The best part of
that is: they don't have to.  And by not trying to please everyone,
there's something out there that's actually fairly good stuff.

